1. No, the original ask has not been fully met. Several variables still do not reflect the transformation they hold. Specifically:

- Lines 88–93:  
  ```java
  byte[] encrypted = new byte[payloadBuffer.readableBytes()];      // raw payload bytes, not yet encrypted
  payloadBuffer.getBytes(0, encrypted);
  if (crypto != null) {
    encrypted = crypto.encrypt(payloadBuffer);                     // result of encryption
    payloadBuffer = Unpooled.wrappedBuffer(encrypted);
  }
  ```  
  • The first `encrypted` is actually the raw payload bytes; it should be renamed (e.g. `payloadBytes`).  
  • The assignment from `crypto.encrypt` should store into `encryptedBytes` (or `encrypted`).

- Line 95:  
  ```java
  String payloadString = ENCODER.encodeToString(encrypted);
  ```  
  • This is the result of Base64‐encoding; it should be named `encodedPayload` or simply `encoded`.

- Lines 97–98:  
  ```java
  byte[] digest = signer.sign(payloadBuffer);
  String digestString = ENCODER.encodeToString(digest);
  ```  
  • The raw signature bytes (`digest`) should be named `signatureBytes` or `signedBytes` (or `digestBytes`).  
  • The Base64‐encoded string should be named `encodedSignature` or `encodedDigest`.

- Lines 133–134:  
  ```java
  byte[] urlEncoded = DECODER.decode(parts[0]);
  byte[] digest = DECODER.decode(parts[1]);
  ```  
  • `urlEncoded` is actually the decoded bytes; rename to `decodedBytes` or `decodedPayloadBytes`.  
  • `digest` should be `decodedSignatureBytes` or `digestBytes`.

- Lines 140–145:  
  ```java
  byte[] message;
  if (crypto == null) {
    message = urlEncoded;
  } else {
    message = crypto.decrypt(Unpooled.wrappedBuffer(urlEncoded));
  }
  ```  
  • `message` should be renamed to `decryptedBytes` (or `messageBytes`) so it reflects the decryption result.

Because none of these variables have been updated, the code still violates the naming conventions requested.  

